(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-blood-disorders-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Blood Disorders Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is extravascular hemolysis?","choices":["Premature red-cell destruction within phagocytes","Red-cell rupture within the circulation","Red-cell loss from bleeding vessels","Failure of red-cell production in marrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the majority of hemolytic anemias the premature destruction of red cells occurs within phagocytes, the same cells that normally remove old red cells; this is called extravascular hemolysis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 39"},{"q":"Which factors make up the common pathway?","choices":["XII, XI, IX and VIII","VII and tissue factor","VIII, IX and XIII","X, V, II and I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The common pathway is factors X, V, II and I, which generate thrombin and fibrin; both the PT (prothrombin time) and the aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) assess it. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Early iron deficiency usually falls in which mean cell volume group?","choices":["Normocytic","Microcytic","Macrocytic","Megaloblastic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Established iron deficiency is microcytic, but EARLY iron deficiency is listed with the normocytic anemias (mean cell volume 80 to 100 fL, femtoliters), before the cells have shrunk. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which bone marrow neoplasm is defined by ineffective hematopoiesis with cytopenias and dysplasia?","choices":["Myelodysplastic neoplasm","Myeloproliferative neoplasm","Acute myeloid leukemia","Plasma-cell myeloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myelodysplastic neoplasms (the World Health Organization's term; myelodysplastic syndromes remains in use elsewhere) show ineffective hematopoiesis with cytopenias and dysplasia, and they can progress to acute myeloid leukemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"On the myeloid branch, which neoplasm arises from the red-cell line?","choices":["Essential thrombocythemia","Polycythemia vera","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Monocytic leukemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the division of hematologic neoplasms by lineage, the red-cell line of the myeloid stem cell gives polycythemia vera and erythroid leukemia. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What happens in immune heparin-induced thrombocytopenia?","choices":["Bleeding from the falling platelet count","Reduced marrow production of platelets","A clonal rise in the platelet count","Thrombosis despite a falling platelet count"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In immune heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, platelet activation causes thrombosis despite a falling count, so a low count does not mean a bleeding tendency here. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where do T-cell precursors develop?","choices":["Bone marrow","Thymus","Germinal centers","Spleen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lymphoid progenitor branches into the B-cell precursor in the bone marrow and the T-cell precursor in the thymus, the origin of T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which absolute neutrophil count defines severe neutropenia?","choices":["Below 200 cells/µL","Below 500 cells/µL","500 to 1,000 cells/µL","1,000 to 1,500 cells/µL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia is graded by absolute neutrophil count: mild 1,000 to 1,500, moderate 500 to 1,000, severe below 500 cells/µL, and agranulocytosis below 200. Severe neutropenia increases the risk of serious infection. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How is a relative white cell count converted to an absolute count?","choices":["Multiply the total white count by the cell's fraction","Divide the cell's percentage by the total white count","Subtract the lymphocyte count from the total white count","Read the cell's percentage straight off the differential"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The absolute count is the white blood cell count times the relative count expressed as a fraction: a total of 8,000 cells/µL with 25 percent neutrophils gives 2,000 neutrophils/µL. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which three morphologic features give etiologic clues in anemia?","choices":["Size, shape and nuclear lobes","Size, hemoglobinization and shape","Color, count and granules","Volume, lifespan and removal site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The morphologic characteristics that provide etiologic clues are red-cell size (normocytic, microcytic or macrocytic), degree of hemoglobinization seen as color (normochromic or hypochromic), and shape. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which leukocyte normally makes up the largest share of the total white cell count?","choices":["Lymphocytes","Monocytes","Neutrophils","Eosinophils"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are normally 40 to 60 percent of the total leukocyte count, the largest share, ahead of lymphocytes at 20 to 40 percent. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is anemia primarily defined by?","choices":["Hematocrit below the laboratory's interval","A red-cell count below the reference range","Hemoglobin below the cutoff for the person","A mean cell volume outside 80 to 100 fL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anemia reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity, and it is defined primarily by hemoglobin below the cutoff for age, sex and pregnancy status; hematocrit only supports. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which pair converts prothrombin to thrombin?","choices":["Factor IXa with factor VIIIa","Factor Xa with factor Va","Tissue factor with factor VIIa","Factor XIIIa with fibrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In thrombin generation, Xa with its cofactor Va converts prothrombin (II) to thrombin (IIa); thrombin then turns fibrinogen (I) into fibrin. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"How does multiple myeloma produce hypercalcemia?","choices":["Altered bone remodeling","Renal injury from monoclonal proteins","Marrow infiltration crowding red cells","Impaired immunity from abnormal antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In myeloma, altered bone remodeling can produce osteolytic lesions and hypercalcemia; the monoclonal proteins and marrow infiltration separately cause renal injury, anemia and impaired immunity. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a neoplasm of which cells?","choices":["Precursor B cells","Mature T cells","Mature B cells","Plasma cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a mature B-cell neoplasm, related to small lymphocytic lymphoma (CLL/SLL). — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 23, 28"},{"q":"Macrocytic anemias often stem from abnormalities that impair what?","choices":["Hemoglobin synthesis","Red-cell survival in the blood","Plasma volume regulation","Erythroid precursor maturation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrocytic anemias often stem from abnormalities that impair the maturation of erythroid precursors in the bone marrow, as in the megaloblastic anemias of vitamin B12 or folate deficiency. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"Which leukocyte binds IgE (immunoglobulin E)?","choices":["Basophil","Neutrophil","Monocyte","Natural killer cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basophils bind IgE (immunoglobulin E) and take part in the parasite response and allergy; few should be seen on a normal complete blood count, so a rise should prompt thought of malignancy. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What kind of disorder is polycythemia vera?","choices":["An erythropoietic response to hypoxia","A fall in plasma from dehydration","A myelodysplastic marrow neoplasm","A clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polycythemia vera is a clonal myeloproliferative neoplasm: an absolute erythrocytosis from clonal proliferation of myeloid cells, listed with chronic myeloid leukemia, essential thrombocythemia and primary myelofibrosis. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 18"},{"q":"What initiates physiologic coagulation?","choices":["Factor XII on contact","Tissue factor with factor VIIa","External bleeding","Thrombin on fibrinogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Physiologic initiation is tissue factor-VIIa activating factors IX and X; external bleeding is not required. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44"},{"q":"How does the Sepsis-3 definition describe sepsis?","choices":["Immature bands above 10 percent of white cells","Neutrophilia during acute bacterial inflammation","Neutropenia below 500 cells/µL with infection","Life-threatening organ dysfunction from infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sepsis-3 defines sepsis as infection-associated, life-threatening organ dysfunction; the band count above 10 percent was a historical criterion of the older systemic inflammatory response syndrome definition. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In leukemia, what can the peripheral white blood cell count be?","choices":["Low, normal or high","High, because blasts spill into the blood","Low, because blasts stay in the marrow","Normal until leukostasis develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The peripheral white blood cell count in leukemia may be low, normal or high, because marrow involvement can suppress normal blood-cell production while malignant cells may or may not spill into the blood. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 22"},{"q":"Approximately what share of whole blood is plasma?","choices":["35 percent","45 percent","55 percent","65 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whole blood is approximately 55 percent plasma and 45 percent formed elements, and plasma itself is about 90 to 92 percent water carrying electrolytes, nutrients, wastes and proteins. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does anemia reduce?","choices":["The blood's plasma volume","The blood's clotting factor supply","The blood's oxygen-carrying capacity","The marrow's white-cell output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anemia reduces the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity, which is why it is defined by hemoglobin below the cutoff for age, sex and pregnancy status. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Hemophilia A is a deficiency of which factor?","choices":["Factor IX","Factor V","Factor VIII","Factor VII"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemophilia A is factor VIII deficiency; hemophilia B is factor IX deficiency. Both factors work together, IXa with its cofactor VIIIa activating factor X. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which organs does leukostasis often involve?","choices":["Lungs and brain","Liver and spleen","Kidneys and bones","Marrow and thymus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukostasis is impaired microcirculation with organ injury, often involving the lungs or the brain. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Multiple myeloma is a malignancy of which cells?","choices":["Precursor B cells","Mature B cells","Myeloid progenitors","Clonal plasma cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Multiple myeloma is a clonal plasma-cell malignancy involving the bone marrow: a malignancy of differentiated, antibody-producing B-lineage cells. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"What does somatic hypermutation in germinal centers produce after antigen activation?","choices":["Immunoglobulin gene rearrangement","Affinity maturation","Maturation arrest","Proof of a malignant clone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After antigen activation, B cells in germinal centers can undergo somatic hypermutation, and selection of the better-binding cells produces affinity maturation. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which condition can blunt the reticulocyte response to hemolysis?","choices":["Rising erythropoietin","Recovery from anemia","Dehydration","Renal disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With hemolysis, erythropoietin and reticulocyte production usually rise when the marrow can compensate, but the response may be inadequate with marrow disease, nutrient deficiency, renal disease or infection. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why does a clonal result alone not establish malignancy?","choices":["Clonal cells carry many different rearrangements","Reactive or premalignant cells can also be clonal","PCR cannot detect receptor-gene rearrangements","Malignant lymphoid cells are usually polyclonal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A clone is a population descended from a common progenitor, and reactive or premalignant populations can also be clonal. The result must be interpreted with morphology, immunophenotype and clinical findings. — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"What does the mean cell volume measure?","choices":["The average hemoglobin mass per red cell","The hemoglobin concentration in packed cells","The average volume of a red cell","The share of blood volume that is red cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mean cell volume is the average volume of a red cell; it sorts anemias into microcytic (below 80 fL, femtoliters), normocytic (80 to 100 fL) and macrocytic (above 100 fL). — Pathophysiology of Blood Disorders SV Fall 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 33"}]};
